WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.920
Do we know what is inside
the software we ship?

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:04.900
Four places flaws hide.

00:00:06.217 --> 00:00:07.767
First, the code we write.

00:00:08.117 --> 00:00:12.067
Broken access control is number
one in the OWASP Top Ten.

00:00:12.337 --> 00:00:14.027
Scanners are good at known patterns.

00:00:14.562 --> 00:00:17.912
They cannot know that an invoice
belongs to another customer.

00:00:18.983 --> 00:00:20.793
Second, the open-source parts.

00:00:21.178 --> 00:00:24.388
Modern software is assembled
from reused components.

00:00:24.878 --> 00:00:26.628
When a flaw is published in one,

00:00:26.858 --> 00:00:29.928
every application shipping
the old version has it too.

00:00:30.763 --> 00:00:33.853
An SBOM, the list of
components in a release,

00:00:34.183 --> 00:00:37.073
is matched against public
vulnerability databases.

00:00:38.483 --> 00:00:40.353
Third, how things are configured.

00:00:40.748 --> 00:00:45.218
Servers and storage are described in
text files, so they are also code.

00:00:45.558 --> 00:00:50.088
One line that opens a storage bucket
is copied to every environment.

00:00:50.313 --> 00:00:53.863
An image built two years ago still
carries two-year-old libraries.

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:56.680
Fourth, too many findings.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:01.220
Scanners report every flaw,
whether or not our software uses it.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:03.930
A severity score alone
keeps the list long.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:05.830
Three questions shrink it.

00:01:06.075 --> 00:01:07.805
Is the flawed code reachable?

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:11.780
Is it being exploited now,
according to the KEV catalogue?

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:15.840
Is exploitation likely soon,
according to EPSS?

00:01:16.917 --> 00:01:18.127
What good looks like.

00:01:18.412 --> 00:01:22.522
A second person reads any change
that decides who may see what.

00:01:22.797 --> 00:01:26.647
Dependency scanning,
with an SBOM for every release.

00:01:26.942 --> 00:01:28.032
Templates and images

00:01:28.462 --> 00:01:29.732
checked before deployment.

00:01:30.022 --> 00:01:31.512
Exploited flaws first.

00:01:31.882 --> 00:01:36.912
NIS2 Article 21 names security
in development and maintenance.

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:40.140
A suggested first step.

00:01:40.425 --> 00:01:41.675
Ask for two numbers.

00:01:42.075 --> 00:01:45.225
Known-exploited flaws that
are reachable in production.

00:01:45.655 --> 00:01:48.305
And how many days the
oldest one has been open.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:51.500
Four places flaws hide.

00:01:51.775 --> 00:01:54.025
Count what is exploited and reachable.

